WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.562
This example creates a small table with several constraints.

00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:08.512
Email is required. The email must match a teaching-level shape check.

00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:11.847
Phone is either NULL or exactly ten digits.

00:00:11.847 --> 00:00:13.742
Email is also unique. Run the valid insert first.

00:00:13.742 --> 00:00:19.022
Then try changing a value to violate one rule and observe PostgreSQL reject it.

00:00:19.022 --> 00:00:26.082
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:30.172
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.172 --> 00:00:34.232
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:40.597
In a real setting such as a product catalog standardizing categories and quarantining invalid rows,

00:00:40.597 --> 00:00:45.372
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:45.372 --> 00:00:47.992
before discussing syntax.

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:53.923
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
